{
  "id": "james-cuda_gittensor-tinyrouter_429",
  "repo_name": "james-cuda/gittensor-tinyrouter",
  "repo_url": "https://github.com/james-cuda/gittensor-tinyrouter",
  "base_commit": "56c319efd865f1236132013b93a453d0ad77b6ff",
  "pr_number": 429,
  "issue_number": 413,
  "issue_title": "[bug] MMLU choice extractor reads the English article \"a\" as choice A \u2014 wrong answers score 1.0 and committed answers score 0.0",
  "issue_body": "**Describe the bug**\n\n`extract_choice_letter`'s committed-answer patterns capture the English indefinite article **\"a\"** as choice **A**, so any answer phrased *\"the answer is a &lt;noun&gt;\"* is graded as if the model had committed to option A.\n\n```python\n# src/trinity/orchestration/reward.py:811\nre.compile(r\"answer\\s*(?:is|:)?\\s*\\(?\\s*([A-J])\\s*(?:[\\).:]|\\b)(?![A-Za-z])\", re.I),\n# src/trinity/orchestration/reward.py:813\nre.compile(r\"\\bfinal\\s+answer\\s*[:=]?\\s*\\(?\\s*([A-J])(?![A-Za-z])\", re.I),\n```\n\nTwo things combine:\n\n1. `[A-J]` under `re.I` also matches lowercase `a`\u2013`j`, so the article `a` is a candidate letter.\n2. The delimiter guard `(?:[\\).:]|\\b)` is satisfied by the **zero-width** `\\b` sitting between `a` and the following space. `(?![A-Za-z])` then inspects that same position, sees a space rather than a letter, and passes.\n\nSo the guard that was added to stop `\"the answer Beats...\"` matching `B` does not stop `\"the answer is a decrease\"` matching `A`:\n\n```\nregex : answer\\s*(?:is|:)?\\s*\\(?\\s*([A-J])\\s*(?:[\\).:]|\\b)(?![A-Za-z])\nmatch : 'answer is a'   captured 'a'\n```\n\nThis is exactly the hazard the module already names one function further down \u2014 the last-line fallback carries a comment about *\"the English article 'A' in prose like 'A nice approach'\"* (`reward.py:874-876`) and is guarded against it. The higher-priority committed-answer patterns, which run first and win, were never given the same guard.\n\nMMLU is one of the three frozen competition benchmarks and its options are usually noun phrases, so *\"the answer is a &lt;noun phrase&gt;\"* is an ordinary way for a model to finish. This misgrades in **both** directions.\n\n**Expected vs actual**\n\nA choice letter must only be extracted when the model actually commits to a letter. Actual: an article is extracted as a letter.\n\n*Direction 1 \u2014 false positive (a wrong answer is graded correct):*\n\n| model output | reference | expected | actual |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `The answer is a decrease in the equilibrium constant.` | `A` | 0.0 | **1.0** |\n| `Thus, the answer is a shift toward the products.` | `A` | 0.0 | **1.0** |\n| `Final answer: a monopoly has no close substitutes.` | `A` | 0.0 | **1.0** |\n| `The correct answer is a stronger acid.` | `A` | 0.0 | **1.0** |\n\n*Direction 2 \u2014 false negative (a correct, explicitly committed answer is thrown away).* Because \"last committed wins\" ranks by match position, a trailing prose sentence outranks a genuine earlier commitment:\n\n| model output | reference | expected | actual |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `\\boxed{C}` + `\\n\\nSo the answer is a straightforward application of the rule.` | `C` | 1.0 | **0.0** (extracts `A`) |\n| `Final answer: D` + `\\nIn short, the answer is a consequence of conservation.` | `D` | 1.0 | **0.0** (extracts `A`) |\n\nThe single-letter English word `I` collides the same way (`I` is inside `A-J`): `The answer is I believe option C` extracts `I`, not `C`.\n\n**To reproduce**\n\nPure/offline, no network and no torch. On `56c319e`:\n\n```bash\npython - <<'PY'\nfrom trinity.orchestration.reward import extract_choice_letter as E, score_text\n\n# 1. the article is captured as a letter\nprint(E(\"The answer is a decrease in pressure.\"))          # 'A'  -- expected None\nprint(E(\"Final answer: a monopoly has no substitutes.\"))   # 'A'  -- expected None\nprint(E(\"The answer is an increase.\"))                     # None -- 'an' is unaffected\n\n# 2. false positive: a wrong answer scores 1.0 against reference A\nprint(score_text(\"mmlu\", \"The answer is a decrease in pressure.\", \"A\"))   # 1.0\n\n# 3. false negative: a committed \\boxed{C} is overridden by trailing prose\nprint(score_text(\"mmlu\", r\"\\boxed{C}\" + \"\\n\\nSo the answer is a straightforward application.\", \"C\"))  # 0.0\n\n# 4. genuine commitments still work (these must not regress)\nprint(E(\"The answer is B.\"), E(\"The answer is (C)\"), E(r\"\\boxed{D}\"))     # B C D\nPY\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\nA\nA\nNone\n1.0\n0.0\nB C D\n```\n\n**Why existing tests miss it:** `tests/test_reward_choice_font_wrappers.py` and the Markdown-emphasis tests from #297/#335 only feed inputs where a real letter is present. No test feeds prose in which the letter-shaped token is an ordinary English word, so nothing pins that a non-committal sentence must extract `None`.\n\n**Suggested fix**\n\nMake the delimiter real instead of zero-width: require an actual `)`/`.`/`:`/`,` or end-of-line/end-of-string after the captured letter, and drop the `\\b` alternative. A committed letter is always followed by punctuation or the end of the line; an article never is.\n\n```python\nre.compile(r\"answer\\s*(?:is|:)?\\s*\\(?\\s*([A-J])\\s*(?:[\\).:,]|$)\", re.I | re.M)\n```\n\nVerified against both the failing and the passing forms:\n\n| text | current | proposed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `The answer is a decrease in pressure.` | `a` | **None** |\n| `Thus the answer is a shift toward products.` | `a` | **None** |\n| `The answer is I believe option C` | `I` | **None** |\n| `The answer is B.` | `B` | `B` |\n| `The answer is (C)` | `C` | `C` |\n| `Answer: C` | `C` | `C` |\n| `The answer is D` | `D` | `D` |\n| `the answer is c.` | `c` | `c` |\n| `Answer: B\\nmore prose follows` | `B` | `B` |\n\nThe `\\bfinal\\s+answer` pattern at `reward.py:813` needs the same treatment \u2014 it has no delimiter requirement at all, only the `(?![A-Za-z])` lookahead, which an article also satisfies. Happy to send a focused PR touching only `reward.py` plus one new test file.\n\n**Environment**\n- Python version: 3.12.9\n- GPU: CPU only\n- Commit hash: `56c319efd865f1236132013b93a453d0ad77b6ff`\n\n**Not a duplicate of:** #297 / #335 (Markdown emphasis around a *real* letter \u2014 `**B**`; both fixed, and both about a letter that is genuinely present), #267 (last-committed-wins across patterns \u2014 that mechanism is correct and is what propagates direction 2 here, but the defect is the article being treated as a commitment in the first place), #285 (`grading_explain` reference-letter reporting), #323 (`parse_verdict` underscore emphasis), #327 (DROP/BBH first-vs-last answer lead). None of them concerns a letter-shaped English word being captured as a choice.\n",
  "merged_at": "2026-07-23T11:20:09.000Z",
